Done. Executed **TASK-021 — Add completion haptic feedback adapter** (the first `pending` task), then stopped without committing per the workflow rules.

## What I built

Formalized the one-line `navigator.vibrate(12)` stub into a real two-channel platform adapter:

- **`prototype/src/lib/haptics.ts`** — channel detection, persisted on/off setting, single-pulse `fireSuccessHaptic()`, and a `useHapticsSetting()` hook.
- **`prototype/src/components/HapticsToggle.tsx` + `.css`** — a Zero-Guilt `role="switch"` settings control, wired into the Little Win card footer.
- **`prototype/src/flows/flowData.ts`** — `fireSuccessHaptic` is now a re-export from the adapter, so all existing call sites (flowKit → three flows + the card) route through it unchanged. Single source of truth, no duplication.

## Acceptance criterion met exactly

> *"Supported iOS devices vibrate once on completion; unsupported devices silently degrade; setting disables haptics."*

- **iOS vibrates once** — iOS Safari doesn't expose the Web Vibration API, so the adapter uses the only web-reachable iOS haptic: a hidden native `<input type="checkbox" switch>` clicked once (one system tick on iOS 17.4+). Android/Chromium use `navigator.vibrate(12)`. One pulse per completion either way.
- **Silent degrade** — channel resolves to `'none'` → no-op; lazy DOM creation (non-iOS never touches the DOM); every fire path wrapped so completion can never break; the toggle shows a plain "not available" line instead of a dead switch.
- **Setting disables** — persisted `localStorage` setting (default on); `fireSuccessHaptic()` returns early when off; subscribe/broadcast store keeps surfaces in sync.

Haptic fires **only** from `HoldToComplete`'s success branch — never on cancel/early-release.

